ash for Amazon Linux and RHEL
=============================

Why this package needs a Python index at install time
-----------------------------------------------------

The package bundles ASH's own wheel and nothing else. Its %post scriptlet creates a
virtualenv under /usr/lib/ash/venv and installs that wheel into it, which resolves
ASH's 27 runtime dependencies from whatever Python index the host is configured for.

This is not an oversight. ASH's own code may ship in a package published by the
project; third-party code may not. Carrying exactly one wheel is the guard that
keeps this package on the right side of that line, because it can be checked by
counting the files under /usr/lib/ash/wheels/ rather than by judging each
dependency one at a time.

Building a self-contained offline package for your own hosts is fine, and the
commands below are the supported way to do it. The line is about what the project
publishes, not about what the bytes are allowed to do.

On a host with no reachable index, stage one before installing:

  pip download automated-security-helper==<version> -d /srv/ash-wheels
  # then, on the target, before dnf install:
  printf '[global]\nno-index = true\nfind-links = /srv/ash-wheels\n' > /etc/pip.conf

Scanners are not installed by this package
------------------------------------------

ASH drives third-party scanners; it does not contain them. After installing:

  ash dependencies install              # everything ASH can provision
  ash dependencies install --tool grype # or one at a time

Files
-----

  /usr/lib/ash/wheels/   ASH's wheel, the package's only payload
  /usr/lib/ash/venv/     created by %post, removed by %postun on erase only
  /usr/bin/ash           wrapper that execs the venv entry point

The venv is built on the target rather than shipped, because a virtualenv records
absolute paths and the interpreter ABI of the machine that created it. %postun checks
$1 = 0 so an upgrade does not delete the venv the new version's %post just built.
